    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm having a major issue with a batch of dark chocolate I tempered this morning. It looks okay right out of the mold, but after sitting for a few hours, it's developed this weird white, dull streaking across the surface. It's like a film has appeared. Can you explain the science behind why this \"bloom\" happens and how I can fix my tempering process to stop it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This is called \"fat bloom,\" which occurs when the cocoa butter in your chocolate separates and rises to the surface, creating that dull, white streaking. This happens when the temperature fluctuates, allowing the cocoa butter to solidify unevenly or recrystallize incorrectly. To prevent this, ensure your tempering process is precise, maintaining a stable temperature during all melting and cooling phases, and keep your finished chocolate stored in a cool, temperature-controlled area.",
      "source": "target",
